README.content:
# crate-ecs

[Русский](README_RU.md)

**An ECS for Haxe game-world simulations: typed data, explicit publication order, prediction and rollback.**

`crate-ecs` helps you write game rules with a clear account of who changes state and when the result becomes visible. Collect damage from every hit, then resolve deaths. Create a character together with its weapon. Correct late input by replaying the same systems. Grant a reward after confirmation, even when presentation has already shown the predicted outcome.

The primary target is **HashLink/C**; **HL/JIT** supports a fast development cycle. The core has no game-engine dependency: the same Plan can serve a client, server and simulation tool. Your application chooses rendering, transport, physics and mathematics; the ECS connects data, execution and state history.

## Why use it

- **The causes of a game result are visible in code.** A system declares reads, writes and their publication boundary, called a `Horizon`. Following systems receive results at a defined boundary. This matters for simultaneous attacks, accumulated forces and rules whose outcome depends on operation order.
- **Composition mistakes are caught during compilation.** Macros check schemas, query compatibility, access rights, link types and handler syntax. Adding a field does not leave a component-name typo to be discovered during a match.
- **Memory is planned with the rules.** Placement capacities, rollback history and output buffers have explicit bounds. A Group protects room for essential participants while optional populations share additional capacity. Memory reports show the buffers of your particular Plan.
- **Compound objects have explicit ownership.** A Prefab creates a tree with reciprocal links, such as a fighter and its weapon. Subtree destruction, attachment and detachment use checked structural operations.
- **Rollback uses your ordinary systems.** Session restores retained state and replays computation with corrected input. You do not need a second implementation of movement or combat that manually reverses the old result.
- **Presentation and external actions have suitable outputs.** Positions come from a typed projection, reversible reactions can follow prediction, and final effects remain queued until the application acknowledges processing them.
- **The hot path is specialized for your game.** Macros turn declarations and iteration into direct column operations. Each row does not contain a separate component object; `foreach` does not allocate a user closure for every participant.

Choose the library when data shapes and world limits are known at compile time, and your game benefits from reproducible execution order and controlled history. Tactical games, arenas, crowd simulations and other projects with their own world rules can use this model. Physics, pathfinding and network protocols connect from outside; the library does not include those subsystems or a content editor.

